1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Modern Marvels is going big and counting down.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
No series has celebrated technology over as many episodes as we have.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:17,000
And now it's time to buckle up and punch it as we count down America's top 10 hottest rides.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:19,000
From the sexy to the speedy.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Mind boggling how fast it is.

6
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
From the retro to the rugged to the reckless.

7
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
These incomparable beasts and beauties will keep you spinning.

8
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:31,680
There's a ton of adrenaline going through your body.

9
00:00:32,180 --> 00:00:35,040
Be there at the finish line when we crown our number one pick.

10
00:00:35,780 --> 00:00:40,480
Now, hot rides on this edition of Modern Marvel's Mega Marvels Countdown.

11
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:54,920
America has always prided itself as a nation on the go.

12
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:58,080
But it's not just about getting from point A to point B.

13
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,560
It's about having a blast along the way.

14
00:01:01,561 --> 00:01:04,100
We love any hot ride that'll blow back our hair,

15
00:01:04,101 --> 00:01:07,780
pump us up, or quicken our pulses.

16
00:01:07,781 --> 00:01:10,120
Only one limitation applies to all the contenders

17
00:01:10,121 --> 00:01:11,380
on our countdown.

18
00:01:11,381 --> 00:01:13,580
They've got to stay earthbound.

19
00:01:13,581 --> 00:01:15,660
So no air or watercraft.

20
00:01:15,661 --> 00:01:19,780
Other than that, anything goes.

21
00:01:19,781 --> 00:01:22,860
Our choice at number 10 is more than a hot ride.

22
00:01:22,861 --> 00:01:24,920
It's boiling hot.

23
00:01:24,921 --> 00:01:27,220
Jay Leno's 1909 Stanley Steamer.

24
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,340
She keeps a century-old classic in his garage,

25
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:33,920
a huge two-building complex,

26
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:36,580
housing enough hot rides to cause a traffic jam.

27
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:40,240
His iconic Stanley Steamer is in a class by itself.

28
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:43,440
Steam cars were quite popular at the turn of the century

29
00:01:43,441 --> 00:01:45,640
because, don't forget, cars back in the early days,

30
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:47,500
you had to crank them, and you had a magneto,

31
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:48,480
and you had to set the choke,

32
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:49,660
and a lot of times they'd backfire,

33
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:50,900
and people would break their arms.

34
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:53,380
Where steam...

35
00:01:53,381 --> 00:01:55,220
Well, steam technology was 100 years old

36
00:01:55,221 --> 00:01:56,640
by the time this car was built.

37
00:01:56,641 --> 00:01:59,180
So steam technology was easily understood.

38
00:01:59,181 --> 00:02:01,320
You heat up water, it makes steam,

39
00:02:01,321 --> 00:02:03,340
it pushes the piston, and you go.

40
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:05,440
Let's open up the hood.

41
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:11,720
Okay, not as pretty as looking at a 289 Mustang

42
00:02:11,721 --> 00:02:14,760
or a 327 Chevy, but this is not your engine.

43
00:02:14,761 --> 00:02:17,060
This is just your boiler.

44
00:02:17,061 --> 00:02:19,360
The reason Jay's hot ride made our countdown

45
00:02:19,361 --> 00:02:21,000
is it's literally just that.

46
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:26,400
Creating the Stanley steam demands red hot temperatures.

47
00:02:26,401 --> 00:02:28,560
This is one of the few cars you can get burned to death

48
00:02:28,561 --> 00:02:31,520
and scalded to death at the same time.

49
00:02:31,521 --> 00:02:33,600
I mean, you're carrying an open flame,

50
00:02:33,601 --> 00:02:34,860
and you're made of wood.

51
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:40,340
Getting a steamer ready to run is a multi-step process,

52
00:02:40,341 --> 00:02:42,460
in which a lot can go wrong.

53
00:02:42,461 --> 00:02:45,040
Like Jay's first try at igniting the gas injectors

54
00:02:45,041 --> 00:02:46,160
that heat up the boiler.

55
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:49,320
Not good.

56
00:02:49,321 --> 00:02:50,700
Okay, that's a bad sign.

57
00:02:56,400 --> 00:03:01,400
Now he's not sure how much vaporized gas is floating around.

58
00:03:01,401 --> 00:03:03,400
Let's take a chance.

59
00:03:03,401 --> 00:03:05,400
There you go.

60
00:03:05,401 --> 00:03:07,400
Exactly what I'm talking about.

61
00:03:07,401 --> 00:03:11,400
As you can see, it has burned.

62
00:03:11,401 --> 00:03:14,400
See, when you go home, your wife knows where you've been.

63
00:03:14,401 --> 00:03:16,400
She can't accuse you of being out or doing something

64
00:03:16,401 --> 00:03:19,400
when your arm is on fire.

65
00:03:19,401 --> 00:03:21,400
Jay has better luck the second time around,

66
00:03:21,401 --> 00:03:24,400
and soon the boiler is warming up.

67
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:29,400
Stanley lets out a loud moan as the metal expands.

68
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:37,620
Next, Jay pushes the car outside to fill up its 30-gallon water tank.

69
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:41,380
I'm slowly building steam pressure. Let's see if we got any. I'll check my whistle.

70
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:44,880
I'm getting there.

71
00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:50,420
See, the nice thing about steam is a gasoline engine might turn 3,500 revolutions per minute.

72
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:54,260
This engine turns about 357 revolutions per mile.

73
00:03:54,261 --> 00:03:57,260
It's just like that.

74
00:03:57,261 --> 00:03:59,260
So when you pull away, dead silent.

75
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:07,760
There's no gears to shift.

76
00:04:07,761 --> 00:04:08,760
There's no transmission.

77
00:04:08,761 --> 00:04:09,760
There's no clutch.

78
00:04:09,761 --> 00:04:11,760
Steam is so powerful, you don't need gears.

79
00:04:14,260 --> 00:04:16,760
Jay's steamer is one of two hot rides on our countdown

80
00:04:16,761 --> 00:04:18,760
with an external combustion engine.

81
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:21,760
Unlike an internal combustion engine,

82
00:04:21,761 --> 00:04:27,340
engine, the Stanley produces its power outside of the engine in its boiler. When

83
00:04:27,341 --> 00:04:32,620
the water boils, it converts from a liquid to a gas. It expands in the process,

84
00:04:32,621 --> 00:04:37,660
creating pressure in a closed system. And that pressure powers the pistons in Jay's

85
00:04:37,661 --> 00:04:44,080
steaming hot ride. Inside a pressurized system, steam is invisible, but when it

86
00:04:44,081 --> 00:04:49,440
vents outside into the cooler air, it condenses into white clouds of water vapor.

87
00:04:49,441 --> 00:04:51,800
This is your throttle right here.

88
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:54,160
And you open your throttle and you go.

89
00:04:56,120 --> 00:04:58,160
Let's see if we can beat the camera car.

90
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:02,860
Just like Jay's huffing and puffing classic from yesteryear,

91
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:06,560
the next hot ride on our countdown also has no gas tank.

92
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:09,560
You'll get a charge out of our pick at number nine,

93
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:12,360
the racy battery-powered Tesla Roadster.

94
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:16,620
When we first presented this hot ride,

95
00:05:16,621 --> 00:05:20,320
It was in keeping with our pledge to showcase pioneering technology.

96
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:24,440
Now Tesla Motors is an established brand,

97
00:05:25,220 --> 00:05:27,980
turning yesterday's cutting edge into the commonplace.

98
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:32,700
I'm driving a prototype of the 2008 Tesla Roadster.

99
00:05:33,380 --> 00:05:35,300
This car is 100% electric.

100
00:05:35,940 --> 00:05:39,320
It goes from 0 to 60 in about 4 seconds

101
00:05:39,321 --> 00:05:42,100
with a top speed of over 130 miles an hour.

102
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:44,620
What's really cool is because it's electric,

103
00:05:44,621 --> 00:05:46,220
it does this with zero emissions.

104
00:05:46,620 --> 00:05:51,360
There's no tailpipe, no emissions whatsoever, and has a range of more than 200 miles.

105
00:05:53,340 --> 00:05:56,760
One look at this chic speed demon tells you why it made our countdown.

106
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:02,060
It was the first vehicle to roll out of Tesla's factory in California's Silicon Valley.

107
00:06:03,660 --> 00:06:08,920
The company's goal was to build a car that wasn't just an eco-friendly alternative to gas-fueled cars,

108
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:13,080
but one that qualified as a hot ride in every conceivable way.

109
00:06:13,081 --> 00:06:19,140
Specifically, we were looking to address the three shortcomings of electric cars in the past,

110
00:06:19,620 --> 00:06:23,560
meaning they had limited range, so we had to give consumers more than enough range.

111
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:24,920
That's the efficiency side of it.

112
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:31,000
Performance, we didn't want consumers to be stuck with a car that underperformed relative to gasoline-powered cars.

113
00:06:31,460 --> 00:06:34,580
And design, we wanted a car that is absolutely gorgeous.

114
00:06:37,060 --> 00:06:39,760
When Tesla's engineers began conceiving the car,

115
00:06:39,761 --> 00:06:43,760
One of their top priorities was to design an innovative power source.

116
00:06:43,761 --> 00:06:47,760
The philosophy of Tesla Motors was to start with the best battery

117
00:06:47,761 --> 00:06:52,760
and to make a battery pack that would allow us to get the range and power that we thought customers would desire,

118
00:06:52,761 --> 00:06:55,760
and then to build the car and the rest of the infrastructure around that.

119
00:06:55,761 --> 00:07:01,760
What we needed was a very stiff structure that was very lightweight

120
00:07:01,761 --> 00:07:08,760
because we needed to somehow incorporate this enormous 900-plus pound battery pack into the skeleton of the car.

121
00:07:08,761 --> 00:07:15,760
So we looked to Lotus Motors over in England, and they taught us a method of building a chassis out of bonded, extruded aluminum.

122
00:07:15,761 --> 00:07:20,760
This allowed us to basically build a chassis that's very lightweight, I can easily pick it up with one hand,

123
00:07:20,761 --> 00:07:27,760
and that's also stiff enough and safe enough for occupant protection safety, while still having an integrated battery carrier.

124
00:07:27,761 --> 00:07:30,760
This is what sits in the back of the Tesla Roadster.

125
00:07:30,761 --> 00:07:33,760
It's what we call the ESS, the Energy Storage System.

126
00:07:33,761 --> 00:07:37,760
It's comprised of 6,831 of these lithium-ion commodity cells.

127
00:07:37,761 --> 00:07:39,760
and unfortunately we can't show you what's inside it

128
00:07:39,761 --> 00:07:40,760
because it's all proprietary.

129
00:07:40,761 --> 00:07:41,760
But if you were to open it up,

130
00:07:41,761 --> 00:07:43,760
you'd find a bunch of these wired up inside.

131
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:51,760
The thousands of lithium-ion cells can deliver up to 400 volts.

132
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:56,760
Their 55 kilowatt hours of storage capacity

133
00:07:56,761 --> 00:07:58,760
give this hot ride its 200-mile range.

134
00:08:02,760 --> 00:08:04,760
After you've driven your 200-plus miles,

135
00:08:04,761 --> 00:08:06,760
you would park the car in the garage at night,

136
00:08:06,761 --> 00:08:10,660
in the garage at night and charge it up using one of these home charging stations.

137
00:08:10,661 --> 00:08:17,660
Simply take the cord, open up the charge port, and plug it right in.

138
00:08:18,860 --> 00:08:21,060
The car takes about three and a half hours to recharge.

139
00:08:21,061 --> 00:08:25,660
That's, you know, if it's completely flat, but certainly you can top it off in less time than that.

140
00:08:25,661 --> 00:08:30,660
And the cost is quite low, so you're looking at between two and three dollars to fully charge the car.

141
00:08:30,661 --> 00:08:36,440
The Tesla's eye-catching design attracts attention wherever it goes.

142
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:40,940
But our definition of a hot ride goes way beyond sexy styling.

143
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:46,800
For us, a hot ride can also be measured by how well it gets you around the battlefield.

144
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:54,100
Let's salute our choice at number eight, the Army's ultimate off-roader, the Bradley Infantry Fighting Vehicle.

145
00:08:54,101 --> 00:09:01,100
You can classify this hot ride as a crossover between an armored personnel carrier and a

146
00:09:01,101 --> 00:09:03,100
full-scale paddle tank.

147
00:09:03,101 --> 00:09:07,100
There's enough room for nine inside, like a roomy minivan.

148
00:09:07,101 --> 00:09:11,100
But don't expect the interior of this beast to be as user-friendly.

149
00:09:11,101 --> 00:09:13,100
It takes a little bit of getting used to.

150
00:09:13,101 --> 00:09:15,100
It's a lot different than getting into any other vehicle.

151
00:09:15,101 --> 00:09:18,100
Just because you've got to watch the heavy hatch, make sure it doesn't close on you or

152
00:09:18,101 --> 00:09:19,100
anything.

153
00:09:19,101 --> 00:09:22,100
And then just kind of snake your way in.

154
00:09:22,101 --> 00:09:24,800
So, it's a little bit different.

155
00:09:24,801 --> 00:09:26,600
It's not too bad once you sit in it.

156
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:31,100
One unique feature of the Bradley fighting vehicle

157
00:09:31,101 --> 00:09:34,100
qualifies it as a hot ride in the truest sense of the phrase.

158
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:39,100
The driver sits virtually on top of the engine block.

159
00:09:41,100 --> 00:09:44,600
You've got a pretty thin wall separating you from the engine pack.

160
00:09:44,601 --> 00:09:46,100
Keeps you really warm.

161
00:09:46,101 --> 00:09:48,100
If you're in the desert, not so good probably.

162
00:09:48,101 --> 00:09:51,100
In the older versions, they didn't really have any cooling system for the crew.

163
00:09:51,101 --> 00:09:53,400
So you were just, if you caught a breeze,

164
00:09:53,500 --> 00:09:54,840
that's what you got.

165
00:09:54,940 --> 00:09:56,500
The Bradley is one of the few entries

166
00:09:56,600 --> 00:09:58,840
in our countdown without tires.

167
00:09:58,940 --> 00:10:01,940
Instead, it's equipped with tank-like tracks.

168
00:10:02,040 --> 00:10:04,840
They provide better traction and make the driving experience

169
00:10:04,940 --> 00:10:06,140
nothing like that of a car.

170
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:13,160
In an automobile, when you were to take a left turn,

171
00:10:13,260 --> 00:10:16,060
you would slow down.

172
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:18,360
But in this, in order to take a left turn,

173
00:10:18,460 --> 00:10:20,960
you have to press on the gas, because that's how the track

174
00:10:20,961 --> 00:10:26,840
track works. Along with the challenges comes some fun. The tracks enable the

175
00:10:26,841 --> 00:10:32,540
tightest turning radius imaginable. That's 27 tons of vehicle turning on a dime.

176
00:10:32,541 --> 00:10:40,580
None of our other hot rides can do this. The Bradley is also our only hot ride

177
00:10:40,581 --> 00:10:46,200
packing heat. Its firepower includes a high-powered machine gun and an anti-tank

178
00:10:46,201 --> 00:10:48,600
missile launcher.

179
00:10:48,601 --> 00:10:51,280
And if anyone fires back, it's nice to know

180
00:10:51,281 --> 00:10:53,340
that the aluminum armor surrounding the crew

181
00:10:53,341 --> 00:10:55,980
can really take a beating.

182
00:10:55,981 --> 00:10:59,920
Able to withstand several rounds of a .50 cal point blank.

183
00:10:59,921 --> 00:11:05,160
We can take anything up to a direct hit from a missile.

184
00:11:05,161 --> 00:11:07,520
As gnarly as this hot ride may be,

185
00:11:07,521 --> 00:11:10,200
even it can't tackle the terrain that the next contender

186
00:11:10,201 --> 00:11:11,360
on our countdown can.

187
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:18,840
We're buckling up and burning rubber,

188
00:11:19,640 --> 00:11:21,540
counting down the top 10 hottest rides

189
00:11:21,541 --> 00:11:23,020
our series has ever featured.

190
00:11:24,040 --> 00:11:25,740
And we're not slamming on the brakes

191
00:11:25,741 --> 00:11:28,300
until we scorch a path to our hottest ride of all

192
00:11:28,301 --> 00:11:29,620
at number one.

193
00:11:31,300 --> 00:11:33,820
Jay Leno's Stanley Steamer got our countdown rolling,

194
00:11:34,660 --> 00:11:35,840
puffing in at number 10.

195
00:11:37,020 --> 00:11:38,740
The battery-driven Tesla Roadster

196
00:11:38,741 --> 00:11:40,060
powered up at number nine,

197
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:43,420
followed by our battle-ready choice at number eight,

198
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:44,980
the Bradley Fighting Vehicle.

199
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:52,900
The hot ride in our number seven spot pushes the limits of off-roading to mountainous new heights, the rock crawler.

200
00:11:55,340 --> 00:12:02,480
Rock crawlers are highly modified trucks or buggies designed to hug terrain that most off-roaders would consider impossible to navigate.

201
00:12:04,060 --> 00:12:11,120
Competitors like these outside of Cedar City, Utah, know that a hot ride doesn't have to be fast to be extreme.

202
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:14,920
It feels like a roller coaster every single course.

203
00:12:14,921 --> 00:12:17,920
A lot of new people come out and watch and they think,

204
00:12:17,921 --> 00:12:19,920
man, this is insane, I can't believe you guys do this.

205
00:12:19,921 --> 00:12:22,920
One thing that all these guys have in common is

206
00:12:22,921 --> 00:12:26,920
just going on a typical trail ride isn't quite enough for them.

207
00:12:26,921 --> 00:12:28,920
They want to be pushed to do something extreme,

208
00:12:28,921 --> 00:12:31,920
not just go out and do the same thing everyone else is.

209
00:12:33,920 --> 00:12:37,920
And you start feeling comfortable being at 70-degree angles

210
00:12:37,921 --> 00:12:41,920
up or down and 50-degree angles sideways.

211
00:12:41,921 --> 00:12:46,260
The competition is different because it's usually a rock pile someplace,

212
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:51,880
and we're just trying to drive up the most extreme thing that the course designer can think of.

213
00:12:55,380 --> 00:13:00,900
Nicole Johnson, a mother of two, has been driving rock crawlers competitively since 2001.

214
00:13:01,620 --> 00:13:05,480
I think it's really exciting, actually, to be behind the wheel of this car.

215
00:13:05,860 --> 00:13:08,760
When you're driving up a big rock, sometimes all you see is sky and clouds.

216
00:13:08,761 --> 00:13:11,900
When you're coming off of something steep, you just see ground,

217
00:13:11,901 --> 00:13:14,300
and you just hope you can run it out and not roll it.

218
00:13:14,301 --> 00:13:18,300
Okay, drive forward. Three wheels.

219
00:13:18,301 --> 00:13:20,600
Nicole, like every driver in rock crawling,

220
00:13:20,601 --> 00:13:23,200
depends on a spotter to act as a second set of eyes

221
00:13:23,201 --> 00:13:24,800
and direct the overall effort.

222
00:13:24,801 --> 00:13:26,620
Go up over there. We're backing down.

223
00:13:26,621 --> 00:13:28,620
Nicole's spotter is her husband, Frank.

224
00:13:28,621 --> 00:13:33,120
There's just little cues that you look for in the suspension

225
00:13:33,121 --> 00:13:36,420
and the way the car is built that you can kind of tell that,

226
00:13:36,421 --> 00:13:39,180
said, OK, we got it, or now we got to back off.

227
00:13:39,181 --> 00:13:39,881
Steer me.

228
00:13:39,882 --> 00:13:41,020
OK, stop.

229
00:13:41,021 --> 00:13:42,720
The driver might get all of the glory,

230
00:13:42,721 --> 00:13:44,920
but it's the spotter that does all the work.

231
00:13:44,921 --> 00:13:46,140
The spotter's the poor guy that's

232
00:13:46,141 --> 00:13:48,320
got to run around the vehicle and pulling on ropes

233
00:13:48,321 --> 00:13:51,420
and stacking rocks and pointing us where we need to go.

234
00:13:51,421 --> 00:13:53,820
The spotters face the greatest dangers in rock crawling.

235
00:14:02,260 --> 00:14:04,920
Especially in the all too likely event of a rollover.

236
00:14:06,420 --> 00:14:11,980
Spotters falling down cliffs and hurting their legs or their knees or any part of their body, really.

237
00:14:12,120 --> 00:14:15,840
If the spotter's in a bad position, they could get run over.

238
00:14:20,220 --> 00:14:26,600
For Nicole and Frank, the secret to making this hot ride as safe as possible is trust and good communication.

239
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:28,660
Drive forward.

240
00:14:28,980 --> 00:14:29,920
It doesn't feel right.

241
00:14:30,820 --> 00:14:31,540
Drive forward.

242
00:14:32,060 --> 00:14:32,760
There you go.

243
00:14:32,761 --> 00:14:33,461
You really have to trust.

244
00:14:33,462 --> 00:14:35,220
Sometimes a car feels really weird.

245
00:14:35,221 --> 00:14:37,200
Feels like you're going to roll when you're really not.

246
00:14:37,201 --> 00:14:38,800
And he can tell if it's going to.

247
00:14:38,801 --> 00:14:39,600
Rear dig down.

248
00:14:39,601 --> 00:14:40,301
Rear dig down.

249
00:14:40,302 --> 00:14:41,002
Down this way.

250
00:14:41,003 --> 00:14:41,760
Yeah.

251
00:14:41,761 --> 00:14:44,280
Your ass hand has to be that way, Nicole.

252
00:14:44,281 --> 00:14:45,600
I'm trying, Frank.

253
00:14:45,601 --> 00:14:48,700
Our way of dealing with it, maybe we yell at each other.

254
00:14:48,701 --> 00:14:49,401
Work left.

255
00:14:49,402 --> 00:14:50,102
Go left now.

256
00:14:50,103 --> 00:14:50,803
Go left.

257
00:14:50,804 --> 00:14:51,580
All the way left.

258
00:14:51,581 --> 00:14:52,420
Work it left.

259
00:14:52,421 --> 00:14:53,520
Left, right.

260
00:14:53,521 --> 00:14:54,280
Go right.

261
00:14:54,281 --> 00:14:55,140
Whatever that is.

262
00:14:55,141 --> 00:14:58,720
But it's all, you know, water under the bridge at that point.

263
00:14:58,721 --> 00:14:59,580
We're here to have fun.

264
00:14:59,581 --> 00:15:00,480
And that's what about.

265
00:15:00,481 --> 00:15:02,220
We had a lot of fights today.

266
00:15:02,221 --> 00:15:03,660
but, you know, we're smiling.

267
00:15:05,520 --> 00:15:06,320
It's all good.

268
00:15:06,460 --> 00:15:07,160
It's good.

269
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:10,240
Beyond good teamwork,

270
00:15:10,680 --> 00:15:12,760
rock crawlers counter this hot ride's dangers

271
00:15:12,761 --> 00:15:13,700
with technology.

272
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:16,680
Your dangers are really high.

273
00:15:16,780 --> 00:15:18,520
If you're not wearing your proper safety harnesses,

274
00:15:18,560 --> 00:15:20,820
if you're not wearing your helmet or fire suits

275
00:15:20,821 --> 00:15:21,620
or anything like that,

276
00:15:22,160 --> 00:15:23,920
the danger is always there.

277
00:15:24,460 --> 00:15:25,760
It's any kind of motorsport.

278
00:15:25,761 --> 00:15:32,760
Rock crawling is a very dangerous sport.

279
00:15:32,761 --> 00:15:34,900
We do treat it with respect.

280
00:15:34,901 --> 00:15:39,740
We make sure that we have the best of the best as far as a suspension seat, the strongest

281
00:15:39,741 --> 00:15:43,300
seat belts that we can get.

282
00:15:43,301 --> 00:15:48,880
And these modified monsters also sport specialized tires available only for competitors, tailor

283
00:15:48,881 --> 00:15:51,740
made for the task.

284
00:15:51,741 --> 00:15:53,160
These are not your normal street tires.

285
00:15:53,161 --> 00:15:55,220
These are 37-inch Maxxis trepidors,

286
00:15:55,221 --> 00:15:57,200
and as you can see just by the tread,

287
00:15:57,201 --> 00:15:59,120
they're ultra-sticky, and this is what keeps us

288
00:15:59,121 --> 00:16:01,280
clinging to those rocks on those side hills,

289
00:16:01,281 --> 00:16:04,120
on those big climbs, this is what gets us up there.

290
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:09,300
But it's more than just enormous tires

291
00:16:09,301 --> 00:16:12,300
that get these buggies around these rocks.

292
00:16:12,301 --> 00:16:14,720
It also takes state-of-the-art suspension systems.

293
00:16:19,660 --> 00:16:22,000
The suspension is known as a four-lane.

294
00:16:22,001 --> 00:16:23,840
As you can see, there are two lower trailing arms

295
00:16:23,841 --> 00:16:24,980
and two upper trailing arms.

296
00:16:25,260 --> 00:16:27,720
Those upper trailing arms are actually what locate the axle

297
00:16:27,721 --> 00:16:31,260
underneath the vehicle, and then the lowers simply provide support.

298
00:16:32,040 --> 00:16:35,240
This particular suspension gives us great movement up and down

299
00:16:35,241 --> 00:16:38,660
for wheel travel and really helps us cling to those hard side hills

300
00:16:38,661 --> 00:16:40,220
and those nasty crawling sections.

301
00:16:41,800 --> 00:16:45,060
And specialized brake controls allow independent operation

302
00:16:45,061 --> 00:16:46,440
of the front and rear brakes.

303
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:56,000
It all adds up to a hot ride that had to make our countdown.

304
00:16:56,001 --> 00:16:59,000
For this bunch, it's the ultimate rush.

305
00:16:59,001 --> 00:17:02,000
When you're hanging off these cliffs and you're, you know,

306
00:17:02,001 --> 00:17:05,000
looking down ready to roll a vehicle, or you're leaning

307
00:17:05,001 --> 00:17:09,000
sideways and having to, you know, pull an awesome save,

308
00:17:09,001 --> 00:17:12,000
trust me, there's a ton of adrenaline going through your body.

309
00:17:12,001 --> 00:17:15,000
I think you have to be a little bit of an adrenaline junkie,

310
00:17:15,001 --> 00:17:21,000
and I can't think of any other sport that I would rather do.

311
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:27,700
Before we pick up our countdown, let's take a quick trip to the Big Apple to acknowledge

312
00:17:27,701 --> 00:17:32,480
one hot ride that didn't make our list, but deserves a mention.

313
00:17:32,481 --> 00:17:36,520
The two-wheelers of New York City's bike messengers.

314
00:17:36,521 --> 00:17:40,960
Only a bicycle can navigate these snarled streets so fast and efficiently.

315
00:17:40,961 --> 00:17:43,560
This business is all about speed and time.

316
00:17:43,561 --> 00:17:47,980
And you know, you want to be able to get around and do things as quickly as possible.

317
00:17:47,981 --> 00:17:52,900
It's the hottest ride in Manhattan, but it's also one of the most dangerous.

318
00:17:52,901 --> 00:17:54,560
I was hit in 2000.

319
00:17:54,561 --> 00:17:59,320
What happened is the truck hit the back of the bike and threw me off the bike, and the

320
00:17:59,321 --> 00:18:03,560
front wheel of the truck actually ran over my shoulder and straight down my arm.

321
00:18:03,561 --> 00:18:06,880
You know, it took me a little while to get over the fear of getting back on the bike,

322
00:18:06,881 --> 00:18:07,880
but you know what?

323
00:18:07,881 --> 00:18:11,860
You have to conquer that fear and just go right back out.

324
00:18:11,861 --> 00:18:16,600
As cool as this hot ride is, we decided there's only enough room for one two-wheeled vehicle

325
00:18:16,601 --> 00:18:18,700
in our countdown.

326
00:18:18,701 --> 00:18:21,160
And that's our choice at number six,

327
00:18:21,161 --> 00:18:24,940
the tiny motorcycle offering titanic thrills, the pocket

328
00:18:24,941 --> 00:18:28,000
bike.

329
00:18:28,001 --> 00:18:31,460
Also known as mini motos, these pint-sized powerhouses

330
00:18:31,461 --> 00:18:34,680
made our cut mainly because they dare to be different.

331
00:18:34,681 --> 00:18:36,040
They're a little motorcycle that

332
00:18:36,041 --> 00:18:38,400
can fit in the backseat of an import car.

333
00:18:38,401 --> 00:18:39,820
They weigh about 40 pounds.

334
00:18:39,821 --> 00:18:43,040
Top speed on a really nice one is about 60 miles an hour.

335
00:18:43,041 --> 00:18:46,340
You're able to do everything that you can on a real motorcycle.

336
00:18:46,341 --> 00:18:49,500
but you're just a lot closer to the ground.

337
00:18:49,501 --> 00:18:51,480
Pocket bikers have been racing seriously

338
00:18:51,481 --> 00:18:54,620
in Asia and Europe since the 1990s.

339
00:18:54,621 --> 00:18:56,840
Today, mini-moto racing is gaining momentum

340
00:18:56,841 --> 00:18:59,660
in the United States, in spite of laws

341
00:18:59,661 --> 00:19:02,960
restricting their use on streets.

342
00:19:02,961 --> 00:19:06,460
Clubs like Pocket Bike GP in Central California

343
00:19:06,461 --> 00:19:09,140
are proving that a hot ride can come in a small package.

344
00:19:11,900 --> 00:19:13,040
Because you're lower to the ground,

345
00:19:13,041 --> 00:19:15,620
the sensation, 30 feels like 130.

346
00:19:15,621 --> 00:19:18,660
I don't even road race anymore, I just do this.

347
00:19:18,661 --> 00:19:21,720
I can crash five times and drive myself home.

348
00:19:23,820 --> 00:19:26,400
Many of its basic components are miniaturized versions

349
00:19:26,401 --> 00:19:28,040
of standard race motorcycles.

350
00:19:29,660 --> 00:19:32,400
Well, here's your kind of standard pocket bike.

351
00:19:33,480 --> 00:19:34,980
All the body work has been removed

352
00:19:34,981 --> 00:19:36,380
to show all the components.

353
00:19:37,540 --> 00:19:39,580
It has pneumatic slick tires,

354
00:19:39,581 --> 00:19:42,120
so you're able to do the incredible lean angles

355
00:19:42,121 --> 00:19:44,640
and have really good traction,

356
00:19:44,641 --> 00:19:47,640
which is important when you're this close to the ground.

357
00:19:47,641 --> 00:19:49,640
It has front and back disc brakes,

358
00:19:49,641 --> 00:19:53,640
so you're able to stop very easily with hardly any effort.

359
00:19:54,640 --> 00:19:57,640
The basic engines that come on pocket bikes

360
00:19:57,641 --> 00:20:01,640
are similar to stuff that you would find in yard equipment,

361
00:20:01,641 --> 00:20:05,640
like a weed eater or something in your garage.

362
00:20:05,641 --> 00:20:08,640
They're two-strokes. They have centrifugal clutch,

363
00:20:08,641 --> 00:20:11,640
so it's one speed. You just give it gas and go.

364
00:20:11,641 --> 00:20:16,640
Pocket bikes range from 2 to 18 horsepower.

365
00:20:16,641 --> 00:20:19,640
They can cost as much as $5,000.

366
00:20:19,641 --> 00:20:23,640
For that, you can get a stronger frame, a lot more speed,

367
00:20:23,641 --> 00:20:26,640
and an even hotter ride.

368
00:20:26,641 --> 00:20:30,640
This is a higher-end bike I purchased to use.

369
00:20:30,641 --> 00:20:32,640
It's a Polini 6.2.

370
00:20:32,641 --> 00:20:36,640
And this is probably between 11 and 12 horsepower,

371
00:20:36,641 --> 00:20:38,640
which, I mean, doesn't sound like much,

372
00:20:38,641 --> 00:20:39,640
but when you think how much it weighs,

373
00:20:39,641 --> 00:20:42,140
it weighs, it's mind boggling how fast it is.

374
00:20:48,640 --> 00:20:50,140
For someone who doesn't know pocket bikes,

375
00:20:50,141 --> 00:20:51,880
they look like toys, like, you know,

376
00:20:51,881 --> 00:20:53,880
you just get on when you do a couple hot laps,

377
00:20:53,881 --> 00:20:55,680
you'll change your mind real fast.

378
00:20:55,681 --> 00:20:56,880
You've never ridden a pocket bike.

379
00:20:56,881 --> 00:21:00,600
You can't fully appreciate the intensity of it.

380
00:21:00,601 --> 00:21:03,300
As awesome as this tiny wonder is,

381
00:21:03,301 --> 00:21:05,800
what if you need a hot ride with the bulk and power

382
00:21:05,801 --> 00:21:08,600
to haul a major load?

383
00:21:08,601 --> 00:21:15,140
That's the specialty of the number five contender on our countdown, the rugged and reliable Ford F-150.

384
00:21:17,860 --> 00:21:21,300
Three million pickups are purchased every year in the United States.

385
00:21:22,220 --> 00:21:26,740
And this burly workhorse has been the best-selling model over the past three decades,

386
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:29,720
reason enough for its place of honor on our countdown.

387
00:21:30,860 --> 00:21:36,420
At Ford's production plant in Dearborn, Michigan, an F-150 comes off the line every 60 seconds.

388
00:21:36,421 --> 00:21:40,420
But before this hot ride reaches your local dealer showroom,

389
00:21:40,421 --> 00:21:44,420
it has to endure a bumpy ride at Ford's Michigan Proving Grounds.

390
00:21:44,421 --> 00:21:47,420
It's difficult just holding 20 miles an hour,

391
00:21:47,421 --> 00:21:49,420
which is the speed limit on this course,

392
00:21:49,421 --> 00:21:54,420
with the bumps and the shakes and how much it throws you around the cab.

393
00:21:54,421 --> 00:21:58,420
But it's a good testament to the control that we have with the F-150

394
00:21:58,421 --> 00:22:00,420
and how it holds you in position,

395
00:22:00,421 --> 00:22:03,420
allows you to control the truck under these incredibly rugged surfaces

396
00:22:03,421 --> 00:22:09,060
rugged surfaces that you're probably not going to encounter in most everyday driving, but

397
00:22:09,061 --> 00:22:11,417
are heavy-duty users as well.

398
00:22:11,418 --> 00:22:16,700
The rough ride is a supreme test of the meeting point between the

399
00:22:16,701 --> 00:22:18,178
truck's cab and box.

400
00:22:18,179 --> 00:22:23,500
The frame is the foundation of the truck, and you've got to have a great

401
00:22:23,501 --> 00:22:26,625
foundation, a really stiff frame to be able to endure this.

402
00:22:26,626 --> 00:22:28,320
But you also have to have really

403
00:22:28,321 --> 00:22:34,560
secure attachments and meeting points for the cab and the truck and obviously with the shape that

404
00:22:34,561 --> 00:22:40,480
we're experiencing here it really tests out how well that cab is attached to the frame.

405
00:22:41,760 --> 00:22:45,520
The meeting point holds but what about the F-150 suspension?

406
00:22:48,240 --> 00:22:52,960
It's taking a real beating and if you watch the back bumper you can see it moving around there.

407
00:22:52,961 --> 00:22:57,280
That suspension is really absorbing the shocks and the load of this really well but it's staying

408
00:22:57,281 --> 00:22:58,440
right in the middle of the course.

409
00:22:58,620 --> 00:22:59,720
The frame is strong.

410
00:22:59,820 --> 00:23:00,880
The suspension is strong.

411
00:23:01,100 --> 00:23:02,860
You can see the truck stays perfectly even

412
00:23:02,861 --> 00:23:03,920
as it goes down the road.

413
00:23:04,200 --> 00:23:05,780
So we want those tires to move,

414
00:23:05,820 --> 00:23:07,620
and you can see them breathing as it comes in here.

415
00:23:08,540 --> 00:23:10,960
And the truck's absorbing all of that energy

416
00:23:10,961 --> 00:23:12,180
as it's coming up to a stop.

417
00:23:12,700 --> 00:23:15,220
This F-150 passed with flying colors.

418
00:23:15,860 --> 00:23:18,060
No surprise for the pickup that muscled its way

419
00:23:18,061 --> 00:23:19,840
to the number five spot in our countdown.

420
00:23:23,520 --> 00:23:24,840
We're racking up the miles

421
00:23:24,841 --> 00:23:26,180
as we continue our countdown

422
00:23:26,181 --> 00:23:28,420
of America's top 10 hot rides.

423
00:23:29,420 --> 00:23:31,960
And our final destination is nearly in sight,

424
00:23:32,260 --> 00:23:35,540
where we'll rev up our sizzling choice at number one.

425
00:23:37,520 --> 00:23:39,920
Jay Leno jump-started our journey at number 10

426
00:23:39,921 --> 00:23:42,120
with his flaming Stanley steamer.

427
00:23:43,180 --> 00:23:46,120
And we sped our way to America's best-selling pickup,

428
00:23:46,620 --> 00:23:48,560
the Ford F-150 at number five.

429
00:23:49,560 --> 00:23:52,820
Now let's salute our brawny pick at number four,

430
00:23:53,560 --> 00:23:55,740
the commercial truck synonymous with toughness.

431
00:23:56,180 --> 00:23:57,300
The Mack truck.

432
00:23:59,400 --> 00:24:02,200
Ask any Mack driver about this iconic big rig,

433
00:24:02,520 --> 00:24:04,380
and it's easy to see why it made our cut.

434
00:24:05,220 --> 00:24:07,360
To me, it's a truck driver's truck.

435
00:24:07,720 --> 00:24:08,860
It's a tough truck.

436
00:24:09,980 --> 00:24:12,440
It's a comfortable work truck,

437
00:24:12,580 --> 00:24:14,340
but they withstand a lot of punishment.

438
00:24:14,820 --> 00:24:16,800
They're good with the abuse, and they take it.

439
00:24:17,340 --> 00:24:19,720
I think the Mack truck, it's American, it's tough.

440
00:24:19,880 --> 00:24:22,380
It'll do anything, and it can get you there.

441
00:24:22,381 --> 00:24:26,000
I think it symbolizes the strength of American trucking.

442
00:24:26,180 --> 00:24:31,320
Mack trucks are named after the pioneering brothers who first produced them.

443
00:24:32,420 --> 00:24:36,360
They've been making their indelible mark on the trucking industry for nearly a century.

444
00:24:37,800 --> 00:24:40,180
And this guy has quite a collection of them.

445
00:24:43,120 --> 00:24:44,880
And I got married in this truck.

446
00:24:47,960 --> 00:24:53,260
Gary Mahan of Basking Ridge, New Jersey, enjoys a committed relationship with these hefty hot rides,

447
00:24:53,540 --> 00:24:55,840
which date back as far as 1916.

448
00:24:56,180 --> 00:25:02,680
We believe this is the oldest unrestored Mac in the country.

449
00:25:02,840 --> 00:25:03,980
It's an AC Bulldog.

450
00:25:05,240 --> 00:25:10,260
Mac produced more than 40,000 Bulldogs between 1915 and 1939,

451
00:25:10,660 --> 00:25:13,840
which became the benchmark by which all other trucks were judged.

452
00:25:16,820 --> 00:25:20,260
The Bulldog earned its canine nickname during World War I.

453
00:25:21,320 --> 00:25:23,640
6,000 Macs served as cargo carriers,

454
00:25:23,641 --> 00:25:28,900
and British soldiers dubbed it the Bulldog because of its pug-nosed hood,

455
00:25:29,240 --> 00:25:32,260
as well as its dogged ability to get supplies to the front.

456
00:25:36,020 --> 00:25:39,100
But it was under the hood where the Bulldog got its bite.

457
00:25:40,880 --> 00:25:42,580
There's no radiator in the front.

458
00:25:43,100 --> 00:25:44,400
It's been relocated.

459
00:25:44,520 --> 00:25:46,700
It was designed and built with the radiator to the rear,

460
00:25:47,360 --> 00:25:51,420
and you've got a four-cylinder engine, 60 horsepower initially,

461
00:25:51,740 --> 00:25:52,920
but had a lot of torque.

462
00:25:53,640 --> 00:25:57,480
After World War I, the Bulldog made its mark stateside,

463
00:25:57,481 --> 00:26:00,440
staking a claim as America's hardest working truck.

464
00:26:00,441 --> 00:26:04,280
It hauled everything from gravel to gasoline,

465
00:26:04,281 --> 00:26:07,680
lighting the way through 30 years of American progress.

466
00:26:07,681 --> 00:26:14,800
Models of the 1920s lit up the night,

467
00:26:14,801 --> 00:26:17,400
not using electricity, but gas.

468
00:26:17,401 --> 00:26:20,600
For the headlights, turn your gas supply on,

469
00:26:20,601 --> 00:26:21,960
acetylene and a tank.

470
00:26:21,961 --> 00:26:27,260
You come forward, open up your headlight bezel, strike it with a match.

471
00:26:29,780 --> 00:26:31,640
Close your bezel and away they go.

472
00:26:38,160 --> 00:26:43,140
Early models of this hot ride also lacked an electric starter, so they had to be hand-cranked.

473
00:26:44,540 --> 00:26:48,220
First thing we have to do is prime each of the four cylinders.

474
00:26:48,480 --> 00:26:51,060
So we give each one a little teaspoonful of gas.

475
00:26:51,960 --> 00:26:55,380
There it goes.

476
00:27:00,600 --> 00:27:05,600
Bulldogs of the 1920s could chug their way up the steepest grades with 10 tons on their backs,

477
00:27:06,180 --> 00:27:10,240
using a powertrain primitive by today's standards, a chain drive.

478
00:27:11,920 --> 00:27:15,300
Chain drive sounds spooky. I don't know what chain drive is.

479
00:27:15,301 --> 00:27:22,180
This is a chain drive feature found on the AC Mack Bulldogs.

480
00:27:22,181 --> 00:27:26,860
Had the little sprocket driving, a bigger sprocket to the rear.

481
00:27:26,861 --> 00:27:31,860
You had the ability to change your speed ratios by changing your sprocket size.

482
00:27:31,861 --> 00:27:37,100
If you're on a highway delivering goods, you'd want a faster speed so you could increase

483
00:27:37,101 --> 00:27:39,500
the size of this small sprocket.

484
00:27:39,501 --> 00:27:45,960
It was very dependable, and it forgave the shocks of the road conditions and soft ground,

485
00:27:46,080 --> 00:27:49,680
so you wouldn't break an axle because the chain would take up the slack.

486
00:27:53,080 --> 00:27:57,500
Beginning in the 1930s, Mack began producing its own diesel engines,

487
00:27:57,820 --> 00:28:00,980
which made the Bulldog more reliable and cheaper to fuel up.

488
00:28:02,260 --> 00:28:06,600
As more competition entered the marketplace, Mack put the Bulldog down,

489
00:28:06,601 --> 00:28:09,600
shepherding in the next era of Mack trucks.

490
00:28:15,600 --> 00:28:18,600
At this point, Mack was heavy in the diesels.

491
00:28:18,601 --> 00:28:21,600
These are the vocational trucks that the customers wanted.

492
00:28:21,601 --> 00:28:24,600
At the end of the 1940s,

493
00:28:24,601 --> 00:28:27,600
Mack's chain drive was finally given the shaft

494
00:28:27,601 --> 00:28:30,600
as an improved technology became the industry standard.

495
00:28:32,600 --> 00:28:34,600
As you can see, the drive shaft brought the power

496
00:28:34,601 --> 00:28:38,260
The driveshaft brought the power from the transmission to a big center differential.

497
00:28:38,860 --> 00:28:45,760
In the differential, you had gears that sent the power out through the axles to your axle hubs on the outside of all of these big wheels.

498
00:28:45,920 --> 00:28:47,460
So it was a much better combination.

499
00:28:48,660 --> 00:28:54,560
Mack kept on trucking through the 20th century, further solidifying its status his trucking's hottest ride.

500
00:29:00,060 --> 00:29:03,860
We got into the R models, a 1985 vintage.

501
00:29:03,861 --> 00:29:06,560
This was Mack's most popular version.

502
00:29:06,561 --> 00:29:08,800
They sold over 400,000 units.

503
00:29:08,801 --> 00:29:10,860
Great truck, very vocational,

504
00:29:10,861 --> 00:29:12,300
did a lot of different things.

505
00:29:12,301 --> 00:29:14,600
And then they sold us the latest and greatest,

506
00:29:14,601 --> 00:29:18,300
which was a 2009 version, 21st century product.

507
00:29:20,140 --> 00:29:22,720
A century of stellar service has elevated the Mack

508
00:29:22,721 --> 00:29:24,020
to a league of its own,

509
00:29:24,021 --> 00:29:26,020
and the number four spot on our countdown.

510
00:29:28,260 --> 00:29:30,560
That leaves just three hot rides left

511
00:29:30,561 --> 00:29:32,960
as we blaze our way toward the checkered flag.

512
00:29:33,860 --> 00:29:42,740
Before we resume our countdown, let's throw a nod to another worthy hot ride that failed

513
00:29:42,741 --> 00:29:44,540
to make our cut.

514
00:29:44,541 --> 00:29:47,480
The Zamboni Ice Resurfacer.

515
00:29:47,481 --> 00:29:52,540
Its top speed is 15 miles per hour, but it stakes a claim as the most popular machine

516
00:29:52,541 --> 00:29:54,880
in sports.

517
00:29:54,881 --> 00:29:59,060
It's equipped with a razor sharp blade that scoops up a fine layer of ice to smooth the

518
00:29:59,061 --> 00:30:00,260
rink surface.

519
00:30:00,261 --> 00:30:05,940
One of the things you really have to watch out for is the snow plugging up between the

520
00:30:05,941 --> 00:30:14,440
conveyors and you want to push this down and that pushes the snow away from the two conveyors

521
00:30:14,441 --> 00:30:18,400
to unplug any snow that might be plugging in down there.

522
00:30:18,401 --> 00:30:23,180
The Zamboni has been described as a cross between a street cleaner, a riding lawnmower

523
00:30:23,181 --> 00:30:28,640
and a brick, but it earns its honorable mention by being the humble hero of America's seven

524
00:30:28,641 --> 00:30:32,480
million ice skaters.

525
00:30:32,481 --> 00:30:35,780
Another ice cold hot ride is so fast and thrilling that it

526
00:30:35,781 --> 00:30:39,580
rocketed its way to number three on our countdown,

527
00:30:39,581 --> 00:30:42,480
the bobsled track at Utah Olympic Park.

528
00:30:45,520 --> 00:30:48,920
Athletes competed on this mile long ribbon of ice at the 2002

529
00:30:48,921 --> 00:30:50,900
Winter Olympics.

530
00:30:50,901 --> 00:30:53,840
Our only hot ride that's faster is the Tesla.

531
00:30:53,841 --> 00:30:56,200
This is the world's fastest track in luge.

532
00:30:56,201 --> 00:30:59,300
Sleds and luge can go up to 90 miles an hour.

533
00:30:59,301 --> 00:31:03,640
Skeletons and bobsleds, it's anywhere between 85 and 90.

534
00:31:06,700 --> 00:31:08,580
The track is made of concrete and steel

535
00:31:08,581 --> 00:31:10,540
and it's on footers and it follows

536
00:31:10,541 --> 00:31:12,320
the contour of the mountain.

537
00:31:14,780 --> 00:31:17,440
The track's designers made it the world's fastest

538
00:31:17,441 --> 00:31:19,640
by incorporating fewer twisting corners

539
00:31:19,641 --> 00:31:22,600
and a straighter path compared to those of other layouts.

540
00:31:22,601 --> 00:31:30,360
But it's also the sleek, ice-cold surface that makes this such a hot ride.

541
00:31:30,361 --> 00:31:34,740
A refrigeration plant near the bottom of the track can keep it frozen even on a hot summer

542
00:31:34,741 --> 00:31:36,500
day.

543
00:31:36,501 --> 00:31:45,280
It produces pressurized liquid ammonia as cold as 4 degrees below zero Fahrenheit.

544
00:31:45,281 --> 00:31:51,120
Pumps circulate 110,000 pounds of it through 54 miles of pipe embedded in the track.

545
00:31:51,121 --> 00:31:54,880
We're currently running at minus 10 degrees Celsius.

546
00:31:54,881 --> 00:31:58,120
We can, on a cold day, shut off the refrigeration,

547
00:31:58,121 --> 00:32:00,620
and it's not needed because the outside air temperature

548
00:32:00,621 --> 00:32:03,120
is actually keeping the ice cold.

549
00:32:05,120 --> 00:32:08,120
The earliest bobsled tracks relied strictly on the winter cold

550
00:32:08,121 --> 00:32:11,120
to stay frozen and were carved directly into the snow,

551
00:32:11,121 --> 00:32:13,120
blanketing the mountain.

552
00:32:16,120 --> 00:32:20,120
Designers used curved wood moldings supported by timber braces

553
00:32:20,121 --> 00:32:24,120
races for the most extreme turns.

554
00:32:28,120 --> 00:32:32,120
Tracks now, like at Utah Olympic Park, are significantly faster.

555
00:32:32,121 --> 00:32:35,120
This is a hot ride you can feel.

556
00:32:39,120 --> 00:32:44,120
When the athletes are coming down the hill, the g-forces they can experience in these large

557
00:32:44,121 --> 00:32:49,120
corners can be four or five times the force of gravity, or four or five g's.

558
00:32:49,121 --> 00:32:52,660
And that sensation is intense.

559
00:32:52,661 --> 00:32:55,560
No motor, no gears, no fuel.

560
00:32:55,561 --> 00:32:59,680
Just gravity and guts make this ride so hot.

561
00:32:59,681 --> 00:33:04,020
That's why it's number three on our countdown.

562
00:33:04,021 --> 00:33:07,360
Our hot ride at number two also runs on a track.

563
00:33:07,361 --> 00:33:10,620
It's a game-changing blast from the past that made America

564
00:33:10,621 --> 00:33:15,160
a giant among nations, the steam locomotive.

565
00:33:15,161 --> 00:33:17,980
This icon of the 19th and 20th centuries

566
00:33:17,981 --> 00:33:21,580
was an indispensable workhorse in the Industrial Revolution

567
00:33:21,581 --> 00:33:26,020
and a major player in the settlement of the American West.

568
00:33:26,021 --> 00:33:28,900
We found a prime example in Ely, Nevada,

569
00:33:28,901 --> 00:33:31,060
where the Nevada Northern Railway houses

570
00:33:31,061 --> 00:33:35,960
its historic steam locomotives in their original engine house.

571
00:33:35,961 --> 00:33:40,700
The locomotive we're going to take out today is Locomotive 93.

572
00:33:40,701 --> 00:33:45,700
She was purchased by the railroad in July of 1909.

573
00:33:45,701 --> 00:33:49,700
Right now, the fireman is preparing the fire to produce the steam.

574
00:33:53,180 --> 00:33:55,160
This firebox is big.

575
00:33:55,520 --> 00:33:58,500
It's about 12 feet deep and about 6 feet wide.

576
00:33:59,160 --> 00:34:01,940
This engine takes a lot of coal to make it run.

577
00:34:05,640 --> 00:34:07,760
Once the boiler builds up enough pressure,

578
00:34:08,340 --> 00:34:10,880
steam generates electricity for the locomotive's lights.

579
00:34:11,760 --> 00:34:15,500
It also runs the air pumps that produce compressed air for the brakes.

580
00:34:15,700 --> 00:34:22,700
Finally, up goes the engine house door, and this historic hot ride chugs out.

581
00:34:35,700 --> 00:34:38,700
We just received a highball, releasing the engine brake.

582
00:34:38,701 --> 00:34:44,000
Placing the power reverse and forward.

583
00:34:44,001 --> 00:34:48,520
And pull it out on the throttle and two blasts on the whistle.

584
00:34:48,521 --> 00:34:51,600
And off we go.

585
00:34:51,601 --> 00:34:53,500
This is copper mining country.

586
00:34:53,501 --> 00:35:02,700
And for nearly 50 years, locomotive 93's job was to haul ore cars.

587
00:35:02,701 --> 00:35:03,840
This is a freight locomotive.

588
00:35:03,841 --> 00:35:05,260
You can tell by the smaller wheels.

589
00:35:05,261 --> 00:35:08,680
It has a lot of track to effort, very little horse power.

590
00:35:08,681 --> 00:35:10,920
The length of effort's about 42,000 pounds,

591
00:35:10,921 --> 00:35:13,080
but the horsepower's only 905.

592
00:35:14,220 --> 00:35:16,080
It's built for power, not for speed.

593
00:35:16,081 --> 00:35:18,820
You just pull about 33 or cars up the hill.

594
00:35:22,600 --> 00:35:24,160
Since this is a working locomotive,

595
00:35:24,161 --> 00:35:25,640
you gotta be careful to let you touch.

596
00:35:25,641 --> 00:35:27,000
Everything's extremely hot.

597
00:35:28,000 --> 00:35:29,860
Just like it should be for a hot ride

598
00:35:29,861 --> 00:35:31,540
this high on our countdown, right?

599
00:35:32,680 --> 00:35:35,000
And it follows that any ride this hot

600
00:35:35,001 --> 00:35:36,740
has to blow off a little steam.

601
00:35:38,680 --> 00:35:44,520
After each run, we do what we call a blowdown.

602
00:35:44,521 --> 00:35:49,240
We do this because sediments build up in the boiler, and we have to clean the boiler out,

603
00:35:49,241 --> 00:35:50,880
get those sediments out of the boiler.

604
00:35:50,881 --> 00:35:55,780
So here goes.

605
00:35:55,781 --> 00:36:02,080
Keep your seat belt fastened just a little while longer, because the only hot ride in

606
00:36:02,081 --> 00:36:05,860
our countdown left to be revealed is our number one pick.

607
00:36:05,861 --> 00:36:10,640
It's a hottie in every sense and named after this history-making fighter plane from World

608
00:36:10,641 --> 00:36:18,700
War II.

609
00:36:18,701 --> 00:36:24,800
We've been putting the pedal to the metal, counting down America's top 10 hottest rides.

610
00:36:24,801 --> 00:36:28,860
And now it's finally time to unveil the ultimate hottie of them all.

611
00:36:28,861 --> 00:36:34,160
But first, let's race through all the entries that made our cut.

612
00:36:34,161 --> 00:36:37,240
Number 10, Jay Leno's Stanley Steamer.

613
00:36:37,241 --> 00:36:41,160
Let's see if we can beat the camera car.

614
00:36:41,161 --> 00:36:44,520
Number 9, the Tesla Roadster.

615
00:36:44,521 --> 00:36:48,280
It goes from 0 to 60 in about four seconds.

616
00:36:48,281 --> 00:36:50,480
Number 8, the Bradley Fighting Vehicle.

617
00:36:54,360 --> 00:36:56,880
Number 7, the Rock Crawler.

618
00:36:56,881 --> 00:36:59,880
There's a ton of adrenaline going through your body.

619
00:36:59,881 --> 00:37:02,760
Number 6, the Pocket Bike.

620
00:37:02,761 --> 00:37:05,460
It's mind boggling how fast it is.

621
00:37:05,461 --> 00:37:09,160
Number five, the Ford F-150.

622
00:37:09,161 --> 00:37:11,260
You've got to have a great foundation,

623
00:37:11,261 --> 00:37:14,100
a really stiff frame to be able to endure this.

624
00:37:14,101 --> 00:37:16,900
Number four, Mack Trucks.

625
00:37:16,901 --> 00:37:20,820
I think it symbolizes the strength of American trucking.

626
00:37:20,821 --> 00:37:25,280
Number three, the bobsled track at Utah Olympic Park.

627
00:37:25,281 --> 00:37:28,060
And that sensation is intense.

628
00:37:28,061 --> 00:37:30,160
Number two, the steam locomotive.

629
00:37:32,760 --> 00:37:36,460
And now, the hot ride born in the 1960s,

630
00:37:36,940 --> 00:37:39,160
but blessed with the timeless mystique of a classic,

631
00:37:40,020 --> 00:37:43,340
our number one pick, the original Ford Mustang.

632
00:37:45,240 --> 00:37:47,100
50 years since its introduction,

633
00:37:47,880 --> 00:37:49,960
this legend of the American auto industry

634
00:37:49,961 --> 00:37:53,200
still has the ability to turn heads and trigger envy.

635
00:37:54,600 --> 00:37:56,640
When it debuted in 1964,

636
00:37:57,460 --> 00:37:59,940
the Mustang was tailor-made for the boomer generation,

637
00:38:00,420 --> 00:38:01,420
then coming of age.

638
00:38:02,160 --> 00:38:05,200
An untapped market, crying out for a car to call its own.

639
00:38:05,700 --> 00:38:08,360
It caught the entire United States' fancy.

640
00:38:08,520 --> 00:38:11,140
Everyone wanted a Mustang. Everyone wanted to be seen in a Mustang.

641
00:38:11,580 --> 00:38:15,980
And sure enough, within the first two years, they sold over a million cars.

642
00:38:16,340 --> 00:38:20,560
The first year sales had set records. No other car had sold in those numbers.

643
00:38:20,561 --> 00:38:26,620
It was all because the car was the right car, the right price, the right time, and it had the right look.

644
00:38:27,840 --> 00:38:31,180
Ironically, this automotive icon was almost the car that wasn't.

645
00:38:31,420 --> 00:38:36,820
Ford General Manager Lee Iacocca couldn't convince the company's executives that the

646
00:38:36,821 --> 00:38:39,420
Mustang was the hot ride they needed to build.

647
00:38:39,421 --> 00:38:44,760
Well, when he first pitched the idea of this youthful, young car, it was basically

648
00:38:44,761 --> 00:38:46,460
summarily thrown out.

649
00:38:46,461 --> 00:38:50,120
And the answer was no, no, over and over again, no.

650
00:38:50,121 --> 00:38:52,860
So that's when he decided, let's leave the Glass House.

651
00:38:52,861 --> 00:38:56,920
Let's get out of there on Michigan Avenue, drive up the street, stop in a little motel

652
00:38:56,921 --> 00:38:59,360
called the Fairlane Motel, run a room,

653
00:38:59,361 --> 00:39:01,180
and think outside the box.

654
00:39:02,700 --> 00:39:05,880
Although Iacocca's design team did just that,

655
00:39:05,881 --> 00:39:09,720
they also used Ford's Dowdy Falcon as their starting point.

656
00:39:09,721 --> 00:39:14,720
This was a typical Falcon, 1963.

657
00:39:14,721 --> 00:39:18,160
The Mustang is based on the car except for the styling.

658
00:39:21,320 --> 00:39:23,780
Basically the same platform as the Falcon.

659
00:39:23,781 --> 00:39:28,120
It's a unit body car, which means it's all one unit.

660
00:39:28,300 --> 00:39:31,300
It doesn't have a separate frame that the body bolts to.

661
00:39:31,301 --> 00:39:35,100
And then they added these torque boxes out here,

662
00:39:35,400 --> 00:39:38,760
which again is just steel plates,

663
00:39:38,980 --> 00:39:42,100
but it added strength to the frame of the car

664
00:39:42,101 --> 00:39:43,820
and kept it from flexing.

665
00:39:45,140 --> 00:39:48,400
Four designers took the Falcon's platform and engine assembly,

666
00:39:48,880 --> 00:39:50,380
then shortened the wheelbase,

667
00:39:51,760 --> 00:39:53,280
lowered the seating position,

668
00:39:53,780 --> 00:39:57,080
reduced the trunk space, and elongated the hood.

669
00:39:58,100 --> 00:40:00,420
They also gave it a well-appointed interior

670
00:40:00,421 --> 00:40:03,560
and wrapped it in its sleek signature body style.

671
00:40:04,480 --> 00:40:08,320
When Iacocca's hot new ride hit the market, it was magic.

672
00:40:08,920 --> 00:40:10,940
It took the country by storm.

673
00:40:11,220 --> 00:40:14,440
There are great stories about people staying outside their dealerships

674
00:40:14,441 --> 00:40:16,440
in crowds that couldn't be contained.

675
00:40:16,720 --> 00:40:19,800
Every car that was in the dealership on the first day selling out.

676
00:40:20,480 --> 00:40:23,040
Boasting a starting price of around $2,500,

677
00:40:23,780 --> 00:40:26,180
the Mustang appealed to just about everyone.

678
00:40:27,780 --> 00:40:29,920
You could buy a Mustang as a hard top,

679
00:40:30,860 --> 00:40:31,560
a fastback,

680
00:40:31,960 --> 00:40:33,060
or a convertible.

681
00:40:34,980 --> 00:40:37,120
One of the keys to the Mustang's success

682
00:40:37,121 --> 00:40:39,100
was that you could make it your own.

683
00:40:40,320 --> 00:40:42,080
And it's very hard today

684
00:40:42,081 --> 00:40:44,160
to find two of these early Mustangs

685
00:40:44,161 --> 00:40:45,160
that are exactly alike.

686
00:40:46,860 --> 00:40:49,080
Any proud classic Mustang collector

687
00:40:49,081 --> 00:40:50,000
will tell you the same.

688
00:40:50,001 --> 00:40:55,000
This is a 1966 convertible.

689
00:40:55,001 --> 00:40:58,000
It has options of power steering.

690
00:40:58,001 --> 00:41:01,000
The most exciting thing is it has air conditioning.

691
00:41:01,001 --> 00:41:05,000
We don't find options like air conditioning on convertibles.

692
00:41:05,001 --> 00:41:12,000
This is a 1967 Fastback, and it's got a neat option in here for people who like to ski.

693
00:41:12,001 --> 00:41:18,000
You can open up the trunk and set your skis in, and they go all the way through to a fold-down rear seat.

694
00:41:18,001 --> 00:41:22,000
This is a 1968 GT.

695
00:41:22,001 --> 00:41:25,940
It's a high-performance 302 motor with a factory four-speed.

696
00:41:25,941 --> 00:41:29,540
The wood option in the car is a deluxe interior.

697
00:41:29,541 --> 00:41:32,140
I have totally rebuilt this car from ground up.

698
00:41:34,880 --> 00:41:36,960
Our love affair with the Mustang is now

699
00:41:36,961 --> 00:41:40,480
a half-century old and still going strong,

700
00:41:40,481 --> 00:41:43,280
making it a slam dunk as the ultimate hot ride

701
00:41:43,281 --> 00:41:45,780
on our countdown.

702
00:41:45,781 --> 00:41:47,840
It and the rest of our worthy contenders

703
00:41:47,841 --> 00:41:51,380
remind us that we're a nation that never sits still.

704
00:41:51,381 --> 00:41:55,220
A hot ride can take many forms, but the common denominator

705
00:41:55,221 --> 00:41:57,380
is the rush we feel when we're off and running.

